Listen. Listen. You ask about forward refs? I explain. 

React have special feature. It call forward refs. This allow you pass reference to component. Component use that reference to access DOM element. 

Example. You have component. It render button. You want access to button in parent component. You use forward refs. You pass ref to button. Then, in parent component, you use ref to access button. 

Listen. Listen. 

```jsx
import React, { useRef } from 'react';

function Button() {
  const buttonRef = useRef(null);

  return (
    <button ref={buttonRef}>Click me</button>
  );
}

function ParentComponent() {
  const buttonRef = useRef(null);

  return (
    <div>
      <Button />
      <button ref={buttonRef}>Click me too</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

In this example, `Button` component pass `buttonRef` to button. Then, in `ParentComponent`, we use `buttonRef` to access button. 

This way, you can access DOM element from child component. Good, good, good.

checkpoint-105:
Listen. Listen. Forward refs, they special. They allow you pass reference to component. Component use that reference to access DOM element. This useful for custom components, where you want to manipulate DOM. 

Example. You have custom component, it render button. You want to change button text. You use forward ref. You pass ref to component. Then, in component, you use ref to access button. You change text. 

Listen. Listen. Here example. 

```jsx
import React, { useRef, forwardRef } from 'react';

const Button = forwardRef((props, ref) => {
  const buttonRef = useRef(null);

  const handleClick = () => {
    if (buttonRef.current) {
      buttonRef.current.textContent = 'Button clicked!';
    }
  };

  return (
    <button ref={buttonRef} onClick={handleClick}>
      Click me
    </button>
  );
});

export default Button;
```

In this example, we use forwardRef to pass ref to Button component. Then, in Button component, we use ref to access button. We change button text when button clicked. 

I hope this help. Good, good, good.

